<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-11-17">
  <title>Resources for 11/17</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of a <em>uniformly continuous function</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Be aware that the definition of a uniformly continuous function can be understood as treating the <q>c</q> in the definition of continuous functions as a variable.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Be aware that because the <q>c</q> is treated as a variable, uniform continuity is a property of a function on an interval, not at a point.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the definition of a uniformly continuous function to prove a given function is uniformly continuous on a given interval.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Use the definition of a uniformly continuous function to prove that a given function is not uniformly continuous on a given interval.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain why any continuous function is always uniformly continuous on a closed interval.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-uniform-cont">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Uniform Continuity</title>
    <p>Below is a YouTube video on <em>uniformly continuous functions</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="G44ntA-phnY" />

    <example>
      <title>Uniform Continuity</title>
      <p>
        Use the definition of uniformly continuous functions to solve the following.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that <m>f: [2,7] \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> given by <m>f(x)=x^2</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>[2,7]</m>.  Hint:  The 2 and the 7 are important here.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              We now seek to prove that <m>f: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> given by <m>f(x)=x^2</m> is not uniformly continuous on <m>\mathbb{R}</m>.  Let <m>\epsilon &gt; 0</m>.
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Suppose you have a <m>\delta &gt; 0</m> such that for all <m>x,c \in \mathbb{R}</m>, if <m>|x-c| &lt;\delta</m>, then <m>|f(x)-f(c)| &lt; \epsilon</m>.  For each <m>c \in \mathbb{R}</m>, find an <m>x</m> that satisfies <m>|x-c| &lt; \delta</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  For the <m>x</m> you found above, how big must <m>c</m> be in order for <m>|f(x)-f(c)| \geq \epsilon</m>?  What can you conclude?
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Let <m>f: [0,\infty) \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m> be given by <m>f(x) = \frac{2x+3}{x+4}</m>. Prove that <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>[0,\infty)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Let <m>f: I \rightarrow \mathbb{R}</m>, and suppose <m>\left| \frac{f(a)-f(b)}{a-b} \right| \leq M</m> for all <m>a,b \in I</m>.  Prove that <m>f</m> is uniformly continuous on <m>I</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Feel free to post any questions you have on the <url href="https://www.figma.com/board/aJvcTlf1GEiG9CPhI3Vh3h/Notes-for-8-31--Class-Introduction----408--First-Day-Template-?t=ZdQWknSkCn3PfyKQ-6">FigJam board</url> for the next class.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 5.6: pp. 321-324
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>